cting and doing the same thing in  different ways you know you you see the  way something is framed by Roger Deakins  and you know I may be right in front of  the clue directly in front of it the  clues in the centre of the frame and I'm  staring at it while I'm talking to him  on the phone  and yet somehow it doesn't really  register but it does unconsciously and  so to me that's what I thought was  intriguing about the part was to take  something that could be so kind of  filled with cliches and put all these  question marks that everybody would have  to be watching while they were watching  me on try to figure out all the other  question marks so that was basically I  didn't and I I also like the idea of a  detective anybody who's good as a  detective has to be infatuated with the  mind of the criminal as well as  empathize with what you know the  families of this situation or anybody  who's been involved in any kind of crime  is going through so I just I like the  idea of somebody who had had his own  past and had to love that mind of the  criminal and I think that just led me  into a dark interesting world but also a  world to walk in when I was dealing with  them I knew somewhere that I loved that  mind that had done something so horrible  and that at the same time to walk in the  mind of the criminal and to know how  much I love them and how much I wanted  to find those girls I loved the fact  that the movie was more of an allegory  and I almost a litmus test that I think  when people come in to this experience I  think that they probably pick and choose  which character they would be if they  were in that situation and Keller  absolutely the last person who would  think we would be but I don't think we  really know with my character I made a  decided effort that I was not reacting  to the fact that my child was dead only  missing because I could not embrace the  fact that she could be dead and that's  about that's what kept me practical I  think that was more interesting to play  then oh my god she's missing so she's  dead Danny reminded me that the  character is not just connected to his  daughter but he has an intense love for  for the Dover's he has an intense love  of everyone around him he loves music so  I looked at it from the state of a  Buddhist approach towards being and then  total interconnected nature and so I  thought well what if I you know and he  has  such a visceral reaction to all of the  lights when he's coming back and it  makes him sick  so I said well what if he has  synesthesia where one stimulation  affects more than one sense and so in  giving him the sense of synesthesia it  gave him you know there's this  empathetic nature to where he you know  that moral quagmire of what do you do  you know versus the responsibility  versus the your nature and at heart our  nature is to remain you know or at least  Franklin's nature was to remain as a  jewel of creation one of God's children  and maintain his hope but it's hard to  keep hope when everything around you  tells you that there's no hope left and  you're just looking to be reinterment  when I was on a plane he said you have  to remember and every person that you  meet there's a little piece of God in  them and that's who you talk to but you  make sure that they see it in you first  so in trying to keep that little peace  of God and continually see it in Keller  and to continually see it in my wife  that continually see it in myself you  know that is what was the nature of of  my process with the character I was  offered the part before dunya was  attached with another director attached  it didn't really interest me it seemed  like some pretty dark water to swim in  and I do as you all know go fairly  deeply into my characters and that made  me nervous  and then as she was saying I too met  Denis this is a beautiful human being a  very very special man and his filmmaking  that you have the pleasure of watching  it's a small portion of who this human  being is and with his decency I felt  they could take his hand and and play  this role in his film that somebody  needed to play or they couldn't make  them move  I think most of the characters have this  moral gray area that makes it really  true because it's tragedy and there's no  right now there's just no right answer  in this circumstance unfortunately so  that opens a lot of doors to just say  okay why is this guy like he is how did  he end up like this and you just really  start to figure it out more about what  happens before the film so in all those  silent moments you at least have a full  human being thinking there and the  makeup with a face I mean you were beat  to a pulp no that was real that was yeah  we've been deep deepening it's worth it  once you see yourself because it puts  you somewhere and it's worth it for the  audience but you know this is a very  private character and have people  touching your face all day it's actually  really hard for me you know before doing  a scene you sort of want to go and the  characters really far back into himself  somewhere and so to be constantly  touched you know I thought I don't think  he liked to be touched either so that  was actually challenging you know  there's a scene where I'm showing you  the pictures of the bloody children's  clothes and the scene was written as  Keller throwing me up against the wall  and strangling me and saying this is  your fault you did this in a really  violent way and on the day of that scene  and by the way we're shooting this  actually very quickly I mean we had an  ample amount of time in a way mostly  waiting for overcast weather in Atlanta  but but we really didn't have a lot of  time to clean certain spots and in the  police station we didn't and I remember  Hugh coming up and saying I just don't  feel right about doing this he's so  physical with with Paul's character and  he's going to be I don't even think they  had not even shot that stuff yet but he  said I really think it's much more  cutting if he just sat there and said to  him this is your fault you know you  wasted time and what ensued was Denis  saying I love that idea and in my  opinion like that some  the best acting I've ever witnessed in  front of me and some of the most  vulnerable acting I've ever seen from an  actor and the stuff that he does in that  scene and and I think what happens when  you have a director who is able to sort  of just steer the ship in the correct  way see that the moment exists and where  it exists and how it how it really can  be two examples of the ways in which  that script was taken away because of  the conversations and there was another  moment I think you will remember that I  couldn't I couldn't get my mind around  this ice bag over Holly's hand and it  didn't I didn't what it like she's  playing a trick with the guy I couldn't  what she heard her hand I wasn't I  wasn't really understanding it and and  then when I did begin to understand it I  just felt it would never work I felt it  would never work because I'd spent so  much brain time on it you know good old  actor thinking and Danny was also very  deft at getting us where we needed to be  when in fact there it was right on the  page in front of us only work with the  director that's in love with you  and that you're in love with because  that's the only way you'll trust him or  her or they will trust you everyone  should work with the director with a  French accent but there were more boobs  in the movie but I will say this is that  I know that acting is not rocket science  but it is an art form and I think you  know when you just personally you're  going from job to job and jump to job  you get on the set and you just want to  hit the result that's it and somewhere  along the line you forget the process  the creative process the getting there  the discovery the leaving yourself alone  the the thinking that you know exactly  what it is and then once you leave  yourself alone you're surprised and it  takes you in another direction but you  trust the director enough  to just fly with it it's such a  beautiful thing to remember what it is  that we do and for people to see the  results of what we do which is something  I think that was quite spectacular we'll  see tomorrow when we looked at the box  office
 

